{
  "dom_id": "step-q126111",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220023/assets/287bde55681f44bd4132fdd5b4777a66d3dba7b16708533d755379c41f35b797.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=-3+4y-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+4y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=1,$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}y(-3+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(-3y+4{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y+4{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{3}+4\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{1}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-3(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})+4(\\frac{27}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =2\\pi (-12+\\frac{104}{3}-20) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
